The '''ninth series''' of ''[[The Great British Bake Off]]'' began on 28 August 2018,<ref name="GBBO2018">{{cite news|url= https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/entertainment-arts-45248320 |title=Great British Bake Off contestants revealed for 2018 |work=BBC|date=20 August 2018 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Shepherd |first1=Jack |title=Everything we know so far about Great British Bake Off 2018 |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/arts-entertainment/tv/news/great-british-bake-off-2018-gbbo-start-air-date-contestants-channel-4-presenters-a8494066.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220618/https://www.independent.co.uk/arts-entertainment/tv/news/great-british-bake-off-2018-gbbo-start-air-date-contestants-channel-4-presenters-a8494066.html |archive-date=18 June 2022 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |accessdate=16 August 2018 |work=The Independent}}</ref> with this being the second series to be broadcast on [[Channel 4]]. The series is presented by [[Noel Fielding]] and [[Sandi Toksvig]], with judges [[Paul Hollywood]] and [[Prue Leith]]. This series saw a few changes to the usual format: the first episode being Biscuit Week (the previous 8 series began with a Cake Week), the documentary inserts were dropped, and the finale included the competitors doing a technical challenge outside the tent for the first time.

The series was won by [[Rahul Mandal]], with [[Kim-Joy]] and [[Ruby Bhogal]] as the runners-up.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/tv-and-radio/2018/oct/30/the-coolest-thing-great-british-bake-off-champion-crowned |title ='The coolest thing': Rahul Mandal crowned Great British Bake Off champion|date=30 October 2018|work=Guardian|accessdate=1 November 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/entertainment-arts-46029963 |title =Great British Bake Off: Final watched by 7.5 million viewers|date=31 October 2018|work=BBC News|accessdate=1 November 2018}}</ref>

